/root/autodl-tmp/symbolic_dynamics/docs/prior_work/legacy/5-riemann_henon/paper_npj/main.tex:269:The structured deviation of the globally scaled GUE surrogate in Figure~\ref{fig:6} is expected and is \emph{not} a critique of the standard RMT result. A single global scale cannot reproduce a logarithmically varying mean density; this is precisely why the literature unfolds the spectrum before comparing correlations \cite{montgomery1973,odlyzko1987}. Our point is narrower and constructive: the deterministic solvers reproduce the mean density directly (under single-point anchoring) without a separate unfolding step, which is the property we set out to test. We have rewritten this section to avoid the earlier, inaccurate suggestion that RMT ``fails'' to describe the zeros.
